  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are mutually exclusive or disjoint if <m>A \cap B = \emptyset </m>. Make a sketch.
      </p>
      <image width="40%">
  <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw (-2,-2) rectangle (4,2)node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A and B are exhaustive if <m> A \cup B =S,</m> Make a sketch.
      </p>
       <image width="40%">
  <latex-image>\begin{tikzpicture}[fill=gray, scale = 0.5]
\draw (-2,-2) rectangle (4,2)node [font=\tiny,text=black,above] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture} </latex-image>
          </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
<p>
  These definitions extend to larger numbers of subsets. For example, given <m> A_1,A_2,...,A_k \subseteq S</m> 
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
         <m>A_1, A_2,...,A_k</m> are mutually exclusive events if <m>A_i \cap A_j = \emptyset</m> for <m>i \neq j</m> (they are pairwise disjoint).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>A_1, A_2,...,A_k</m> are exhaustive events if <m>A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots \cup A_k=S</m>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>
<p>We are interested in computing the probability of an event A in our sample space. We can approximate the probability by repeating the experiment n times. 
Let N(A) be the number of times an outcome of the experiment is in the event (subset) A. Then we can approximate the probability of A as 
  </p>
  <exercise workspace="0.5in">
  <statement> 
    <p><m> P(A)\approx</m>
    </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
    <p>
     which is called the <alert> relative frequency </alert> of the event A in these n repetitions of the experiment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note that if n is small and a few of us do this experiment we
      may get wildly different answers. However as n gets larger 
      and larger, this fraction will tend to stabilize and we call it the <alert> probability of event A </alert>. 
      You can think of it as
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          (Probability of A) <m>P(A)=</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workshpace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Suppose we have a fair, six sided die and we want to know the probability of the event, E that it will show an even number. 
      Find the sample space S and E. What do you expect for P(E)?  
      </p>
    </statement>
      
    </exercise>
    <p>
      Now we give the formal definition of a probability function.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-probability">
    <title>Probability</title>
       
      <statement>
        <p>
          Probability is a real-valued set function, P, 
          that assigns to each event A in the sample space S,
          a number P(A), called the probability of the event A
           such that the following properties are satisfied:
           <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m> P(A)\geq 0</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>P(S)=1</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If <m>A_1,A_2,A_3,...</m> are events and 
                <m>A_i \cap A_j = \emptyset, i \neq j,</m> (mutually disjoint)
                then
<md>P(A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots \cup A_k) = P(A_1)+P(A_2)+ \cdots + P(A_k)</md>

for each positive integer k and
<md>P(A_1 \cup A_2 \cup A_3 ...) = P(A_1)+P(A_2)+ P(A_3) +...</md>
for any countable (possibly infinite) number of events.
              </p>
            </li>
           </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sometimes you can use mathematics to determine what the probability function should be under given conditions.
          For example in the 6-sided die example, if the die is fair, then

    <md>P(1)=P(2)=P(3)=P(4)=P(5)=P(6)=</md>


    Let <m>E = \{ 1,2 \}, F = \{3,4\}.</m> Let G be the set of odd outcomes.
    </p> 
    <p>
    Find <m>P(E), P(F), P(E \cup F), P(E\cap F), P(G),P(G\cup F).</m>

        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      <alert>Equally Likely Outcomes</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose we have a random experiment with m outcomes and 
    sample space <m>S=\{e_1,e_2,...,e_m\}</m>. If each of the 
    outcomes in S has the same probability of occurring
    (e.g. roll a fair die), then we say that each of the m
     outcomes are equally likely. In that case,
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>P(e_i)=</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      This makes it easy to determine the probability of an event:
      If <m>A \subseteq S</m> is an event, with h outcomes, then
      <md> P(A)=</md>
    </p>
