   <exercise>
    <introduction>
    
      <p>
        Draw one card at random from a standard 52 card deck. The sample space is the collection of 52 cards. Assume that each card is equally likely to be chosen (i.e., that a card is drawn `at random'). Thus the probability function assigns a probability of <m>\frac{1}{52}</m>
        to each of the outcomes. Let 
    <ul>>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>A=\{x \mid x \hbox{ is a jack, queen, or king}\}</m>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>B=\{x \mid (x \hbox{ is a }9, 10, \hbox{ or jack) AND RED}\}</m>
      </p>
    </li> 
    <li>
      <p>
        <m>C=\{x \mid x \hbox{ is a club}\}</m>
      </p>
    </li> 
    <li>
      <p>
     <m>D=\{x \mid x \hbox{ is a diamond, a heart, or a spade}\}</m>
      </p>
    </li>
     </ul>
     Determine the following probabilities.
     </p>
</introduction>
    
